Inside aggregation stages, prefix field names with $ to reference document values:
{ $project: { full: { $concat: ["$first", " ", "$last"] } } }
A double-dollar $$variable references a named variable (NOW, ROOT, CURRENT, or pipeline-defined).

Same syntax as find filter:
{ $match: { status: "paid", total: { $gte: 50 } } }
Always put $match as early as possible in the pipeline. When $match is the first stage, MongoDB can use indexes to skip non-matching documents entirely. Later in the pipeline it can't.
// GOOD — index on status used
db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $group: { ... } }
])
// BAD — has to run group on all docs, then filter
db.orders.aggregate([
{ $group: { ... } },
{ $match: { status: "paid" } }
])
The query planner sometimes pushes $match up automatically, but don't rely on it — write the pipeline correctly.

db.users.aggregate([
{ $project: {
name: 1,
email: 1,
_id: 0,
age_group: { $cond: [{ $lt: ["$age", 18] }, "minor", "adult"] }
}}
])
Excludes everything not mentioned (except for computed fields). For mostly-keep-but-tweak, prefer $addFields.

{ $addFields: { name_upper: { $toUpper: "$name" }, processed_at: "$$NOW" } }
{ $set: { discounted: { $multiply: ["$price", 0.9] } } } // alias for addFields
Doesn't remove other fields.

db.orders.aggregate([
{ $project: {
user_id: 1,
item_count: { $size: "$items" },
total: 1,
month: { $dateTrunc: { date: "$at", unit: "month" } },
summary: { $concat: [
"$status", " ",
{ $toString: "$total" }, " — ",
{ $dateToString: { date: "$at", format: "%Y-%m-%d" } }
]}
}}
])

$group collapses input documents into one document per group key.
{ $group: {
_id: "$user_id", // group key
total_spent: { $sum: "$total" },
order_count: { $sum: 1 },
avg_total: { $avg: "$total" },
first_order: { $min: "$at" },
last_order: { $max: "$at" }
}}
_id is the group key. It can be:
a field reference: _id: "$user_id"
a compound: _id: { user: "$user_id", month: { $dateTrunc: { date: "$at", unit: "month" } } }
a literal: _id: null (collapse to a single global aggregate)

db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $group: {
_id: "$user_id",
revenue: { $sum: "$total" },
orders: { $sum: 1 }
}},
{ $sort: { revenue: -1 } },
{ $limit: 10 }
])

"Top product per category":
db.products.aggregate([
{ $sort: { category: 1, sales: -1 } },
{ $group: {
_id: "$category",
top: { $first: "$$ROOT" }
}},
{ $replaceRoot: { newRoot: "$top" } }
])
$$ROOT references the whole current document.

{ $sort: { total: -1, at: -1 } }
{ $limit: 20 }
{ $skip: 40 }
{ $count: "matching_count" }
$sort followed by $limit is optimised — the server only keeps the top-N in memory instead of sorting everything.
$count returns a single document { matching_count: N }. For aggregations, this is cleaner than countDocuments.
Trick: for sort + limit to be efficient over big collections, the sort field needs an index. Otherwise the entire input is sorted in memory (subject to the 100 MB stage memory limit; pass { allowDiskUse: true } to spill).

$unwind deconstructs an array, emitting one document per element.
db.orders.insertOne({
_id: "ox",
user_id: 1,
items: [
{ p: "p1", qty: 3 },
{ p: "p2", qty: 1 }
]
})
db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { _id: "ox" } },
{ $unwind: "$items" }
])
// emits:
// { _id: "ox", user_id: 1, items: { p: "p1", qty: 3 } }
// { _id: "ox", user_id: 1, items: { p: "p2", qty: 1 } }

{ $unwind: { path: "$items", preserveNullAndEmptyArrays: true } }
{ $unwind: { path: "$items", includeArrayIndex: "items_idx" } }
preserveNullAndEmptyArrays: true keeps documents where the array is missing or empty (otherwise dropped).

db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $unwind: "$items" },
{ $group: {
_id: "$items.p", // product id
units_sold: { $sum: "$items.qty" }
}},
{ $sort: { units_sold: -1 } }
])
$unwind followed by $group is the canonical pattern for "compute totals across array elements".

{ $lookup: {
from: "orders",
let: { uid: "$_id" },
pipeline: [
{ $match: { $expr: { $eq: ["$user_id", "$$uid"] } } },
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $sort: { at: -1 } },
{ $limit: 5 }
],
as: "recent_orders"
}}
The pipeline form lets you filter/sort/limit the joined documents — far more efficient than fetching all then dropping.

These terminal stages write the pipeline result back to a collection.
// $out — fully replaces target collection
db.orders.aggregate([ /* ... */, { $out: "daily_revenue" } ])
// $merge — upsert/merge into existing collection
db.orders.aggregate([
/* ... */,
{ $merge: { into: "daily_revenue", on: ["day"], whenMatched: "merge", whenNotMatched: "insert" } }
])
$merge is the way to build incremental materialised views — Post 06.

db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: {
status: "paid",
at: { $gte: ISODate("2026-05-01"), $lt: ISODate("2026-06-01") }
}},
{ $group: { _id: "$user_id", spent: { $sum: "$total" } } },
{ $sort: { spent: -1 } },
{ $limit: 10 },
{ $lookup: { from: "users", localField: "_id", foreignField: "_id", as: "user" } },
{ $unwind: "$user" },
{ $project: { _id: 0, user_name: "$user.name", spent: 1 } }
])

db.orders.aggregate([
{ $match: { status: "paid" } },
{ $group: {
_id: { $dateTrunc: { date: "$at", unit: "day" } },
revenue: { $sum: "$total" },
orders: { $sum: 1 }
}},
{ $sort: { _id: 1 } }
])
